+\paragraph{(d) Verify and append tokens.}
+When the root for $x$ finishes all $T$ loops, the decoder takes the
+greedy next token $a$ at that root, appends $a$ to the output, and
+adds $a$ to the suffix index. If the root has a child $xa$, the
+decoder makes that child the new root with score one, keeps the
+child's subtree and its completed loops, and discards the old root's
+other branches. It then recomputes path scores from $xa$ and adds
+suffix drafts from $xa$. If the child is absent, the decoder starts a
+new draft tree at $xa$. In both cases the output uses the full-depth
+greedy prediction. Thus LoopFill produces the same sequence as greedy
+decoding whenever both compute the same final predictions.
+
+A new root that has already finished all $T$ loops can supply the next
+output token in the same step. In Figure~\ref{fig:loopfill-trees}(d),
+the final prediction at the position processing \LFtok{like} is
+\LFtok{to} in step~3. At the end of that step, the positions processing
+\LFtok{to}, \LFtok{join}, and \LFtok{Lean} each need one more loop,
+while the position processing \LFtok{Club} needs two more loops.
+The first three positions finish together in step~4 and predict
+\LFtok{join}, \LFtok{Lean}, and \LFtok{Club}, respectively.
+The decoder appends those three tokens in order. In step~5 the position
+processing \LFtok{Club} finishes its last loop and predicts
+\LFtok{\textbackslash n}. Algorithm~\ref{alg:loopfill} summarizes
+one loop step.
